            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the polynomial curve - a straight line, a parabola, a cubic... - that passes as close as possible to a set of points, and gives its coefficients.\n\nDegree 1 is a straight line: the two coefficients are its slope and its offset. Degree 2 is a parabola, a + b x + c x x, and fits anything that curves once (a thrown object's path, an arc of a gesture). Higher degrees follow more wiggles - and, past a point, start following the noise instead of the shape.\n\nUse it to find a trend in a window of data (and subtract it), to measure how fast something is rising, to smooth a curve to its overall shape, or to calibrate a sensor whose response bends. np.polyval draws the curve from the coefficients.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.polyfit <degree: int>\nDefault: degree 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.polyfit 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe values of the points. Every input is fitted and sent. A 2-D array fits each column separately, one set of coefficients per column.\n\nx:\nWhere the points are, held. Without it they are at 0, 1, 2 ...\n\ndegree (int box, also an inlet):\nThe degree of the curve. It needs at least one more point than its degree.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncoefficients:\nHighest power first: for degree 2, c b a in c x x + b x + a. Ready for np.polyval.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: five noisy points rising by about 2 each step, from about 1. The line through them has slope 1.99 and offset 1.04.\n\nBottom: five points near 0 1 4 9 16 - the squares. Degree 2 gives about 0.96 0.11 -0.01: close to 1 x x + 0 x + 0. A metro re-sends both five times a second; the round nodes only tidy the numbers for reading.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyval evaluates the curve. np.linalg.lstsq fits any combination of functions, of which a polynomial is one. np.interp passes exactly through every point instead.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the polynomial curve - a straight line, a parabola, a cubic... - that passes as close as possible to a set of points, and gives its coefficients.\n\nDegree 1 is a straight line: the two coefficients are its slope and its offset. Degree 2 is a parabola, a + b x + c x x, and fits anything that curves once (a thrown object's path, an arc of a gesture). Higher degrees follow more wiggles - and, past a point, start following the noise instead of the shape.\n\nUse it to find a trend in a window of data (and subtract it), to measure how fast something is rising, to smooth a curve to its overall shape, or to calibrate a sensor whose response bends. np.polyval draws the curve from the coefficients.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.polyfit <degree: int>\nDefault: degree 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.polyfit 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe values of the points. Every input is fitted and sent. A 2-D array fits each column separately, one set of coefficients per column.\n\nx:\nWhere the points are, held. Without it they are at 0, 1, 2 ...\n\ndegree (int box, also an inlet):\nThe degree of the curve. It needs at least one more point than its degree.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncoefficients:\nHighest power first: for degree 2, c b a in c x x + b x + a. Ready for np.polyval.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: five noisy points rising by about 2 each step, from about 1. The line through them has slope 1.99 and offset 1.04.\n\nBottom: five points near 0 1 4 9 16 - the squares. Degree 2 gives about 0.96 0.11 -0.01: close to 1 x x + 0 x + 0. A metro re-sends both five times a second; the round nodes only tidy the numbers for reading.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyval evaluates the curve. np.linalg.lstsq fits any combination of functions, of which a polynomial is one. np.interp passes exactly through every point instead."
        },
